Upgrading from Texy 3
*********************

.[perex]
Texy 4 is rebuilt from the ground up: it first parses the text into a document tree and only then creates HTML from it. For you, that means typography and security for both Markdown and Texy syntax, source text tools and a cleaner API. Your configuration from version 3 keeps working, and it tells you what to rewrite. Here you will find what has changed and how to upgrade.


Installation
============

```shell
composer require texy/texy:^4.0
```

Texy 4 requires PHP 8.4 or newer and the `mbstring` extension. If you use the Latte integration, you need Latte 3.1.7 or newer. If you generate `id` attributes for headings (`headingIds`), you also need the `intl` extension. Texy 3 managed without it using iconv; Texy 4 throws an exception without it.


What Keeps Working
==================

Code that only configures Texy and converts text keeps working unchanged, apart from a few [constants and one property |#Where the Settings Moved] that are gone entirely. Every old name, however, triggers an `E_USER_DEPRECATED` notice that tells you what to replace it with:

```php
$texy = new Texy;
$texy->imageModule->root = '/images/';
$texy->headingModule->top = 2;
$html = $texy->process($text);
```

```
Class Texy is deprecated, use Texy\Texy instead.
$texy->imageModule->root is deprecated, use $texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl instead.
$texy->headingModule->top is deprecated, use $texy->config->headingTop instead.
Texy::process() is deprecated, use Texy::toHtml() instead.
```

So you can migrate your code step by step, following what the notices report. A setting that has no replacement in version 4 is reported with a warning, and reading results that lived on modules in version 3, such as `$texy->headingModule->title`, throws an exception with instructions. The same goes for `addReference()` of the link and image modules, because it takes `Texy\Link` and `Texy\Image` objects, which no longer exist in version 4. The compatibility layer goes away in version 5.

Behavior changes made through handlers and custom syntax added through `registerLinePattern()`, on the other hand, no longer work and need to be rewritten. The [last section |#Extensions] describes how.


What Changed in the Output
==========================

Some things look different by default. Go through them if your CSS or JavaScript relies on the shape of the HTML:

- **An image with a caption** is a `<figure>` with `<figcaption>` instead of a `<div class="figure">` with `<p>`. `$texy->htmlOutput->figureTag = 'div'` and `figureClass = 'figure'` bring back the old form.
- **A code block** is `<pre class="language-php"><code>` instead of `<pre class="php">`, as syntax highlighting libraries expect.
- **An image on a line of its own is always a `<figure>`**, even without a caption, and it does not get a `max-width` style. In version 3 it needed a caption for that.
- **Non-breaking spaces after one-letter words** are added only for languages that have them configured, that is Czech, Slovak and Polish. Version 3 inserted them in every language, so English output, for example, will change. Texy hyphenates long words only in Czech and Slovak.
- **Images have no URL prefix.** Version 3 prepended `images/`; in version 4 you set the prefix in `$texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl`.
- **Safe mode also disables images with captions and image definitions**, not just inline images. You allow images in comments by calling `Texy\Configurator::safeMode($texy, images: true)`.
- **A link written only as the name of a reference**, `[name]`, is disabled by default, because it was easily confused with text in brackets. `$texy->allowedSyntax['link/reference'] = true` turns it on.
- **HTML attributes are not checked against HTML 4.** Whatever `allowedTags` allows gets through, see [configuration |configuration#Allowed HTML Tags, Classes and Styles].

And a few changes in how text is read:

- **A tab is a single space character**; it no longer expands to eight spaces. Text that mixed tabs and spaces and relied on alignment to eight columns may be parsed differently.
- **Soft hyphens typed directly in the text are removed**, always. If you want to keep a soft hyphen, write the entity `&shy;`.
- **Text in invalid UTF-8 throws** a `Texy\InvalidInputException` right at the start.
- **A block of a type Texy does not know, such as `/--php`, is no longer a code block.** It is displayed exactly as written, including the `/--php` and `\--` lines. Write code in a language as <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;php</code> or `/--code php`, or handle the type with your own function, see [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax#A New Block Type in Texy Syntax].
- **Obsolete syntax in stored texts** triggers an `E_USER_DEPRECATED` notice: the image `[* small.jpg | large.jpg *]`, of which only the small image remains, and a description or modifier in a link definition, `[ref]: url description .(title)`, which Texy ignores. Write a link to a large image as `[* small.jpg *]:large.jpg`.
- **The `/--texysource` block is disabled by default.** You turn it on with `$texy->allowedSyntax['block/texysource'] = true`. Headings inside it count among the document's headings.


Where the Settings Moved
========================

In version 4, settings are divided by what they concern: `$texy->config` determines the meaning of the text, `$texy->htmlOutput` the shape of the HTML, and `$texy->htmlPolicy` with `$texy->urlPolicy` what is allowed. All of them are described in [configuration |configuration].

|---
| Texy 3 | Texy 4
|---
| `$texy->allowed` | `$texy->allowedSyntax`
| `$texy->allowed['typography']`, `['longwords']` | `$texy->config->typography`, `hyphenation`
| `$texy->allowedTags`, `allowedClasses`, `allowedStyles` | `$texy->htmlPolicy->…`
| `$texy->urlSchemeFilters` | `$texy->urlPolicy->linkPattern`, `imagePattern`
| `$texy->alignClasses` | `$texy->htmlOutput->textAlignClasses`, `verticalAlignClasses`
| `$texy->obfuscateEmail` | `$texy->htmlOutput->obfuscateEmail`
| `$texy->nontextParagraph` | `$texy->htmlOutput->imageParagraphTag`
| `$texy->mergeLines` | `$texy->config->mergeLines`
| `$texy->typographyModule->locale` | `$texy->config->locale`
| `$texy->longWordsModule->wordLimit` | `$texy->config->hyphenationWordLimit`
| `$texy->headingModule->top`, `generateID`, `idPrefix` | `$texy->config->headingTop`, `headingIds`, `headingIdPrefix`
| `$texy->headingModule->balancing`, `moreMeansHigher` | `$texy->config->headingBalancing`, `headingMoreMeansHigher`
| `$texy->headingModule->title`, `TOC` | `$document->findFirst(HeadingNode::class)?->getTitle()`, `$document->find(HeadingNode::class)`, see [configuration |configuration#Headings]
| `$texy->figureModule->tagName`, `class` | `$texy->htmlOutput->figureTag`, `figureClass`
| `leftClass`, `rightClass` for images and images with captions | `$texy->htmlOutput->floatClasses['left']`, `['right']`
| `$texy->imageModule->root`, `fileRoot` | `$texy->htmlOutput->imageBaseUrl`, `imageBasePath`
| `$texy->linkModule->root`, `forceNoFollow`, `shorten` | `$texy->htmlOutput->linkBaseUrl`, `linkNoFollow`, `shortenUrls`
| `$texy->phraseModule->tags` | `$texy->htmlOutput->phraseTags`
| `$texy->horizLineModule->classes` | `$texy->htmlOutput->horizontalRuleClasses`
| `$texy->htmlModule->passComment` | `$texy->htmlOutput->passHtmlComments`
| `$texy->htmlOutputModule->indent`, `lineWrap`, … | `$texy->htmlOutput->indent`, `lineWrap`, …
| `$texy->emoticonModule->icons`, `class` | `$texy->config->emoticons`, `$texy->htmlOutput->emoticonClass`
| `$texy->listModule->bullets` | `$texy->allowedSyntax['list/decimal']`, `['list/roman']`, `['list/alpha']`, a switch for each kind of numbering; the characters are fixed
| `$texy->phraseModule->linksAllowed` | `$texy->allowedSyntax['phrase/link']`
| `$texy->typographyModule->locales` | `$texy->config->locales`, where a language's entry has the form `['quotes' => […]]`
| `$texy->linkModule->addReference($name, $link)` | `$texy->config->linkResolver`, a function that returns the address for a name, see [configuration |configuration#Links Written by Name]
| `$texy->imageModule->addReference($name, $image)` | an image defined in the document, or an address set by a pass over `ImageNode`

Removed without replacement: `$summary`, `$tabWidth`, `$removeSoftHyphens`, `headingModule->levels` (the levels of underline characters are fixed), `figureModule->widthDelta` and `requireCaption`, `tableModule->oddClass` and `evenClass`, `scriptModule->separator`, `linkModule->imageClass`, `imageModule->linkedRoot` and `defaultAlt`, and image emoticons (`emoticonModule->root` and `fileRoot`). Emoticons are output as Unicode characters.

Constants have new names: instead of `Texy::ALL` and `Texy::NONE`, write `true` and `false`; `HeadingModule::DYNAMIC` and `FIXED` are now the enum cases `Texy\HeadingBalancing::Dynamic` and `Fixed`; `Texy::VERSION` is `Texy::Version`.

Anything that is gone entirely fails right away with an error, without a notice. Delete the output mode constants `Texy::HTML5`, `Texy::XHTML1_STRICT` and the others; the output is always HTML5. The static property `Texy\Configurator::$safeTags` is now the constant `SafeTags` and cannot be changed. So adjust the tags allowed in comments only after calling `safeMode()`, through `$texy->htmlPolicy->allowedTags`.


Text Conversions
================

|---
| Texy 3 | Texy 4
|---
| `process($text)` | `toHtml($text)`
| `process($text, true)`, `processLine($text)` | `toInlineHtml($text)`
| `processTypo($text)` | `applyTypography($text)`
| `toText()` without a parameter, for the last converted text | `toText($text)`, for a parsed document `renderText($document)`
| `getDOM()` | `parse($text)`, which returns the document tree
| `checkURL()` | `$texy->urlPolicy->isLinkAllowed()`, `isImageAllowed()`

Markdown, both reading it and converting to it, is handled by the separate package [texy/markdown |markdown].


Extensions
==========

This is where the biggest change lies. Version 3 changed behavior through handlers and events; version 4 replaces them with extension points that follow the same rules for Markdown and Texy syntax:

|---
| Texy 3 | Texy 4
|---
| `addHandler('image', …)` and other element handlers | a renderer, `$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer()`
| `$invocation->proceed()` | `$previous($node)`
| `addHandler('afterParse', …)`, `beforeParse` | a pass, `$texy->addPass()`
| `addHandler('script', …)` | a directive, `$texy->config->directiveHandler`
| `addHandler('block', …)` | a `/--` block type, `$texy->config->delimitedBlockHandler`
| `registerLinePattern($handler, $pattern, $name)` | `addInlineSyntax($name, $pattern, $handler)`, the name comes first
| `registerBlockPattern($handler, $pattern, $name)` | `addBlockSyntax($name, $pattern, $handler)`
| `Texy\HtmlElement` | `Texy\Output\Html\Element`
| `$texy->protect()` | `new Texy\Output\Html\Raw($html)`
| a custom module | registering syntaxes, passes and renderers; modules are internal

The old methods throw a `LogicException` that names the replacement. In version 3, a handler that added an attribute to images looked like this:

```php
$texy->addHandler('image', function (Texy\HandlerInvocation $invocation, Texy\Image $image, ?Texy\Link $link) {
	$element = $invocation->proceed($image, $link);
	$element->attrs['loading'] = 'lazy';
	return $element;
});
```

In version 4, it is a renderer for the image node. Instead of `proceed()`, it calls `$previous($node)`, which renders the image as usual:

```php
use Texy\Nodes\ImageNode;
use Texy\Output\Html;

$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (ImageNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, Closure $previous) {
	$el = $previous($node);
	$el->attrs['loading'] = 'lazy';
	return $el;
});
```

A custom syntax function does not return HTML but a document node, and it rejects the matched text by returning `null`. Neither the `$againTest` parameter nor `$parser->again` is needed anymore. Inner text is parsed by `$context->parseInline()` or `$context->parseBlock()` instead of `$el->parseLine()` and `$el->parseBlock()`.

Check the regular expressions you carried over from version 3. The `Texy\Patterns` constants have new names and the old ones are gone: `MODIFIER` is `Modifier`, `MODIFIER_H` is `ModifierHAlign`, `MODIFIER_HV` is `ModifierHVAlign`, `LINK_URL` is `LinkUrl` and `CHAR` is `Letter`. They are written for expressions delimited by `~` and do not work in an expression delimited by `#`. Texy adds the `u` flag itself, and a group that did not take part in the match has the value `null` in `$matches` instead of an empty string.

When building HTML, watch out for two changes. The second parameter of the `Html\Element` constructor holds only attributes; you add text with the `setText()` method. And `new Texy\Modifier('[foo]')` no longer parses the string but turns it into an ID. To create a modifier from a string, use `Texy\Modifier::parse('[foo]')`.

All of this is described in detail, with examples, on the pages [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers] and [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax].

